\title{The Anti-Fibonacci Exclusion Rule:\\Degeneracy, Exact Asymptotics, and an Extremal Graph Connector}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We analyze the recurrence obtained by starting with $A_0=A_1=1$ and defining each subsequent term to be the least positive integer unequal to the sum of the preceding two terms. Although this rule has been proposed as an additive-avoidance counterpart to the Fibonacci recurrence, its exclusion set at each step is a singleton. We prove that the least positive integer outside such a singleton is $2$ only when the forbidden value is $1$, and is $1$ otherwise. It follows by two-step induction that $A_n=1$ for every $n\geq0$. Consequently, the displayed prefix $1,1,2,4,7,11,16,\ldots$ is incompatible with the recurrence; the normalized sequence $A_n/n^2$ converges to $0$, not $1/4$; and its value at the millionth index is exactly $10^{-12}$. We also associate a graph to the recurrence by joining time indices whose values sum to $2$. This graph is complete and therefore has exactly $\binom n2$ edges on $n$ vertices. The results expose a general specification issue in greedy additive constructions: a meaningful global avoidance process must state its candidate universe, reuse policy, monotonicity requirements, and complete forbidden set.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The Fibonacci recurrence $F_{n+2}=F_{n+1}+F_n$ turns a local additive instruction into exponential growth, with consecutive-term ratios approaching the golden ratio. This behavior motivates an ``anti-Fibonacci'' construction that avoids addition rather than enforcing it.

Consider the literal proposal $A_0=A_1=1$, with $A_{n+2}$ equal to the smallest positive integer not equal to $A_{n+1}+A_n$. A proposed prefix is
\[
1,1,2,4,7,11,16,\ldots,
\]
and proposed behavior includes $A_n\sim n^2/4$ and a nonconvergent neighboring-term ratio.

The recurrence supports none of these conclusions. At every step exactly one integer is prohibited. Unless it is $1$, the least legal positive integer is $1$. Because the initial pair sums to $2$, the process immediately returns $1$ and remains there. We compute the minimizing operation, derive the closed form and analytic consequences, and then encode an additive relation among indices as an extremal graph.

\section{Definitions and elementary structure}
Write $\mathbb N=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$ and let $\mathbb Z_{>0}$ denote the positive integers.

\begin{definition}[Least positive value avoiding one sum]
For $x,y\in\mathbb N$, define
\[
L(x,y)=\min\{m\in\mathbb Z_{>0}:m\neq x+y\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Singleton-exclusion formula]
For all $x,y\in\mathbb N$,
\[
L(x,y)=
\begin{cases}
2,&x+y=1,\\
1,&x+y\neq1.
\end{cases}
\]
Moreover, $L(x,y)>0$, $L(x,y)\neq x+y$, and every positive $m\neq x+y$ satisfies $L(x,y)\leq m$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $x+y\neq1$, then $1$ is admissible and no positive integer is smaller. If $x+y=1$, then $1$ is excluded while $2$ is admissible. Positivity, avoidance, and minimality follow in the corresponding case.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Literal anti-Fibonacci recurrence]
Define $A:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ by $A_0=A_1=1$ and
\[
A_{n+2}=L(A_{n+1},A_n)
=\min\{m\in\mathbb Z_{>0}:m\neq A_{n+1}+A_n\}.
\]
No distinctness, non-reuse, or monotonicity condition is imposed.
\end{definition}

\section{Collapse of the recurrence}
\begin{theorem}[Constant-Sequence Theorem]
For every $n\in\mathbb N$, $A_n=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use two-step induction. The base values are given. If $A_n=A_{n+1}=1$, their sum is $2$, so the singleton-exclusion formula gives $A_{n+2}=L(1,1)=1$.
\end{proof}

Thus $(A_n)_{n\geq0}=(1,1,1,\ldots)$.

\begin{corollary}[Failure of the displayed prefix]
The recurrence does not generate $1,1,2,4,7,11,16,\ldots$. In particular, $A_2\neq2$ and $A_3\neq4$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The constant-sequence theorem gives $A_2=A_3=1$.
\end{proof}

Directly, the first disputed term is itself forbidden: after $1,1$, the forbidden sum is $2$. The displayed finite list also has differences $0,1,2,3,4,5$ and agrees, at its shown indices, with
\[
C_n=1+\frac{n(n-1)}2,
\]
whose leading coefficient is $1/2$, not $1/4$.

\section{Exact analytic behavior}
\begin{theorem}[Quadratic normalization]
As $n\to\infty$ through positive integers,
\[
\frac{A_n}{n^2}\longrightarrow0.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The closed form gives $A_n/n^2=1/n^2$. Given $\varepsilon>0$, choose $N>1/\sqrt{\varepsilon}$. For $n\geq N$, one has $0\leq1/n^2<\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Impossibility of the proposed limit]
The sequence $A_n/n^2$ does not converge to $1/4$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
It converges to $0$, real limits are unique, and $0\neq1/4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Millionth normalized value]
\[
\frac{A_{1{,}000{,}000}}{(1{,}000{,}000)^2}
=\frac1{1{,}000{,}000{,}000{,}000}=10^{-12}.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Substitute $A_{1{,}000{,}000}=1$ and square the index.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Consecutive-term ratios]
For every $n\geq0$, $A_{n+1}/A_n=1$. Hence the ratios converge to $1$ and do not oscillate between $1$ and $2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Both numerator and denominator equal $1$.
\end{proof}

\section{An extremal graph connector}
\begin{definition}[Sum-to-two index graph]
For $n\in\mathbb N$, let $G_n$ be the simple graph with vertex set $\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}$. Distinct vertices $i$ and $j$ are adjacent exactly when $A_i+A_j=2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Complete-Graph Theorem]
For every $n$, the graph $G_n$ is the complete graph on $n$ vertices.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For any distinct $i,j$, the constant-sequence theorem gives $A_i+A_j=1+1=2$. Thus every possible pair is adjacent.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact edge count]
For every $n$,
\[
|E(G_n)|=\binom n2=\frac{n(n-1)}2.
\]
For $n\geq2$, its edge density is $1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Edges of a complete graph are precisely the unordered pairs of distinct vertices. There are $\binom n2$ such pairs, which is the maximum possible number.
\end{proof}

This construction generalizes: for a sequence $(B_n)$ and target $t$, join $i$ and $j$ when $B_i+B_j=t$. Edge counts record additive representations and clique structure records collections whose pairwise sums hit the target. Here repetition forces maximal density; a repaired increasing sequence would generally produce a sparser graph.

\section{Algorithms and numerical diagnostics}
\begin{algorithm}[Direct recurrence simulation]
Given $N\geq0$, initialize $A_0=A_1=1$. At each step compute $s=A_n+A_{n+1}$ and output $2$ if $s=1$, otherwise $1$. Generation through index $N$ takes $O(N)$ time and $O(N)$ output storage, or $O(1)$ auxiliary storage if only the final value is retained. Correctness is exactly the singleton-exclusion formula.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Closed-form evaluator]
Given any $N\geq0$, return $1$. The constant-sequence theorem proves correctness. Time and auxiliary space are $O(1)$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Complete-graph edge counter]
Given $n\geq0$, return $n(n-1)/2$. The Complete-Graph Theorem and edge-count corollary prove correctness. The algorithm uses $O(1)$ arithmetic operations, apart from integer bit complexity.
\end{algorithm}

Representative normalized values are
\[
\begin{array}{c@{\quad}c@{\quad}c}
\toprule
n&A_n&A_n/n^2\\
\midrule
1&1&1\\
10&1&10^{-2}\\
100&1&10^{-4}\\
1{,}000&1&10^{-6}\\
1{,}000{,}000&1&10^{-12}\\
\bottomrule
\end{array}
\]
They approach zero monotonically for positive $n$.

\section{Density and the choice of universe}
Claims about ``the complement'' require an ambient set. The value set of the literal sequence is $\mathcal V=\{1\}$, which has natural density $0$; its complement in the positive integers has density $1$. The set of sums formed from values at distinct indices is $\{2\}$ and also has density $0$. A changing historical sumset from a repaired recurrence is a third object and can behave differently.

A density claim must therefore specify the ambient universe, whether multiplicity matters, whether equal indices may be used, whether all historical terms participate, and whether the complement concerns values or sums.

\section{Diagnosing and repairing the specification}
The collapse occurs because minimization ranges over all positive integers while the forbidden set has one element. A nontrivial greedy construction must settle four issues.

First, the candidate universe might consist of all positive integers, unused positive integers, or integers larger than the previous term. Only the latter choices force movement. Second, the forbidden family might contain only the latest sum, all adjacent historical sums, or the full pairwise sumset
\[
\{A_i+A_j:0\leq i,j\leq n\}.
\]
Third, reuse and monotonicity must be explicit. Fourth, one must state whether equal indices are allowed in sums.

A plausible research template is
\[
B_{n+1}=\min\left\{m>B_n:m\notin\{B_i+B_j:0\leq i,j\leq n\}\right\}.
\]
This is not asserted to reproduce the displayed prefix. It instead demonstrates a well-specified, strictly increasing, global-avoidance rule. Its study should begin with existence of admissible values, followed by bounds, sumset structure, and asymptotics.

\section{Fixed points and a set-theoretic criterion}
Regard $(x,y)$ as a state and update it by
\[
T(x,y)=(y,L(y,x)).
\]
The state $(1,1)$ is fixed. Whenever $x+y\neq1$, the update's second component is $1$. Since nonnegative solutions to $x+y=1$ are only $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$, the minimizing operation is strongly attracted to $1$, and the prescribed positive initial state never enters the exceptional branch.

Set-theoretically, the admissible set is $\mathbb Z_{>0}\setminus\{x+y\}$. Its minimum depends only on whether the deleted singleton contains $1$. More generally, for a forbidden set $S_n$, the least admissible positive integer can exceed $k$ only if every integer in $\{1,\ldots,k\}$ is forbidden or removed from the candidate universe. A singleton can therefore never force a choice above $2$. This criterion explains structurally why the proposed rule cannot generate sustained growth.

\section{Applications and future work}
The immediate application is specification testing for recursive objects. Before a large simulation, one should check boundary cases, smallest admissible choices, fixed points, and short cycles. This is particularly important in greedy algorithms, where an omitted non-reuse condition can collapse an intended construction.

Future work should repair the definition before studying asymptotics. The recurrence must be reconciled with the displayed prefix, whose visible pattern suggests coefficient $1/2$ rather than $1/4$. A richer replacement may choose the least unused integer outside a restricted historical sumset, connecting additive combinatorics with greedy algorithms. One can then investigate existence, monotonicity, counting functions, and sum-free structure.

The graph bridge should also be retained. For a repaired sequence, additive adjacency may yield informative edge densities, clique numbers, and forbidden subgraphs. Density questions must wait until the universe is fixed: complements of value sets and complements of sumsets are different objects.

\section{Conclusion}
The literal exclusion rule has the exact solution $A_n=1$ for all $n\geq0$. Consequently, the advertised prefix is not generated, quadratic normalization tends to $0$, the millionth normalized value is $10^{-12}$, and consecutive ratios equal $1$. The graph joining indices whose values sum to $2$ is complete with $\binom n2$ edges.

The broader lesson is constructive. Additive avoidance can support substantial mathematics only after its forbidden family and candidate universe are specified. With those choices explicit, asymptotics, density, algorithms, and graph invariants become meaningful rather than artifacts of an ambiguous rule.

\end{document}

